The philosophy of ALP has been a guideline in other enactments also such as the Central Excise Act, the Companies Act, etc. Even in the Income-tax Act, this concept has been incorporated in other places also such as in sections 40A(2), 44C, 80HH, 80-IA, etc.

Sub-section (1) of section 92C stipulates that ALP shall be determined by any of the following methods, being the most appropriate method :—

(a)comparable uncontrolled price method;

(b)resale price method;

(c)cost plus method;

(d)profit split method;

(e)transactional net margin method;

(f)such other method as may be prescribed by the Board.

BACKGROUND OF APPEAL BEFORE THE TRIBUNAL IN CASE OF MENTOR GRAPHICS (NOIDA) (P.) LTD. v. DY. CIT

3. In the background of the foregoing discussion, the Tribunal’s decision in case of Mentor Graphics (Noida) (P.) Ltd. v. Dy. CIT [2007] 165 Taxman 28 (Delhi)(Mag.) laying down some fundamental principles concerning ALP, can be examined.

3.1 FACTS OF THE CASE - Mentor Graphics (Noida) (P.) Ltd. (‘MG’) is a domestic company, incorporated on February 27, 1998. It is an wholly- owned subsidiary of IKOS Systems Inc. - a USA company (AE). The Indian subsidiary is engaged in the business of software development and also renders marketing systems services to AE. It filed its return of income for the assessment year 2002-03 on October 31, 2002, declaring total income at INR 3,99,080 for the financial year 2001-02. The income disclosed included profit from export of computer software to its parent company for which deduction under section 10A was claimed.

The business of MG comprises of development of software as instructed by the AE. The software developed by the MG is used by the parent AE in-house for integrating the same with other software components deve-loped by the parent AE itself. The whole software, in turn, supports the hardware manufactured by the parent, and sold as a package in the open market by the parent AE. Therefore, the MG’s business is limited to providing services of software development support.

The case of the MG before the Assessing Officer was that its software supported hardware manufactured by the parent company. It assumed low business risks and employed only routine tangible assets. It did not own or develop any valuable or non-routine intangibles. All intangibles, including discoveries, improvement, inventions and conceivable secrets, etc., were the sole and exclusive property of the parent company.

The accounts and auditor’s report of the taxpayer showed that the taxpayer had carried the following transactions with its AEs :—

